{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XfiNyqgs74UYqRLnyGiqBp","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ACL Rolling Review review form scoring scale overall assessment 2025 2026\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Review Form – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform\"},{\"title\":\"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Reviewer Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"How ARR works – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewing\"},{\"title\":\"ACL Rolling Review Data Collection (ARR-DC) - ARR Data Collection\",\"url\":\"https://arr-data.aclweb.org/\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Statistics\",\"url\":\"https://stats.aclrollingreview.org/\"},{\"title\":\"Incentives 2026 – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/incentives2026\"},{\"title\":\"ACL ARR 2025 May | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/group?id=aclweb.org%2FACL%2FARR%2F2025%2FMay\"},{\"title\":\"🎪 ACL 2025 Reviews Are Out: To Rebut or Flee? The Harsh Reality of NLP’s \\\"Publish or Perish\\\" Circus | CSPaper Forum\",\"url\":\"https://forum.cspaper.org/topic/27/acl-2025-reviews-are-out-to-rebut-or-flee-the-harsh-reality-of-nlp-s-publish-or-perish-circus\"},{\"title\":\"ACLRollingReview (@ReviewAcl) / Posts / X\",\"url\":\"https://x.com/ReviewAcl\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about the ACL Rolling Review scoring scales:\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation. The excitement score is orthogonal to the assessment of the soundness of the paper.\n\nIf you give a low Soundness score without finding any major faults, this means that your review is not a faithful explanation of your recommendation, and you need to revise it.\n\nThe search results also reference an Overall Assessment scoring scale with specific tier descriptions, including \"Award-worthy (top 2.5%)\" at the 5.0 level, though the complete scoring details were not fully captured in the search results.\n\nFor more detailed information about the specific scoring scales and review form structure, you may want to visit the official ACL Rolling Review website directly at aclrollingreview.org/reviewform or aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1446a884-2136-4659-bcf0-843e85ca00ce","uuid":"78ef70c1-72cf-4444-9640-908bcf403bb1","timestamp":"2026-05-17T08:58:58.809Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"ACL Rolling Review review form scoring scale overall assessment 2025 2026","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01DFqa6VE4nfcxotq3vUgHTC","content":[{"title":"Review Form – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"},{"title":"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/"},{"title":"ARR Reviewer Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines"},{"title":"How ARR works – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewing"},{"title":"ACL Rolling Review Data Collection (ARR-DC) - ARR Data Collection","url":"https://arr-data.aclweb.org/"},{"title":"ARR Statistics","url":"https://stats.aclrollingreview.org/"},{"title":"Incentives 2026 – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/incentives2026"},{"title":"ACL ARR 2025 May | OpenReview","url":"https://openreview.net/group?id=aclweb.org%2FACL%2FARR%2F2025%2FMay"},{"title":"🎪 ACL 2025 Reviews Are Out: To Rebut or Flee? The Harsh Reality of NLP’s \"Publish or Perish\" Circus | CSPaper Forum","url":"https://forum.cspaper.org/topic/27/acl-2025-reviews-are-out-to-rebut-or-flee-the-harsh-reality-of-nlp-s-publish-or-perish-circus"},{"title":"ACLRollingReview (@ReviewAcl) / Posts / X","url":"https://x.com/ReviewAcl"}]},"Based on the search results, here's what I found about the ACL Rolling Review scoring scales:\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation. The excitement score is orthogonal to the assessment of the soundness of the paper.\n\nIf you give a low Soundness score without finding any major faults, this means that your review is not a faithful explanation of your recommendation, and you need to revise it.\n\nThe search results also reference an Overall Assessment scoring scale with specific tier descriptions, including \"Award-worthy (top 2.5%)\" at the 5.0 level, though the complete scoring details were not fully captured in the search results.\n\nFor more detailed information about the specific scoring scales and review form structure, you may want to visit the official ACL Rolling Review website directly at aclrollingreview.org/reviewform or aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines."],"durationSeconds":4.761654020999995}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011xSNnwXabCLoyUkipq28sj","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"site:aclrollingreview.org reviewform scoring criteria overall assessment\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Review Form\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Reviewer Guidelines\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Area Chair Guidelines\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"Response to ACL 2023 Questions\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/ACL-2023-session/\"},{\"title\":\"Action Editor Form\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/actioneditorform\"},{\"title\":\"Authors Guidelines\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors\"},{\"title\":\"CALL FOR PAPERS – ACL Rolling Review\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/cfp\"},{\"title\":\"How ARR works – ACL Rolling Review\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewing\"},{\"title\":\"Area Keywords at ARR\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/areas\"},{\"title\":\"ARR SAC Guidelines\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/sacguidelines\"}]\n\nI found relevant information about the ACL Rolling Review (ARR) scoring criteria and overall assessment. Here's what the search results show:\n\n## Scoring Criteria\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation.\n\nThe Overall assessment score is an explicit recommendation for the outcome of this paper, if it were committed to an *ACL venue. This is a composite score reflecting your assessment of soundness, excitement, and also other factors like novelty and impact.\n\n## Soundness Criteria\n\nFor main vs Findings papers: the main criteria for Findings are soundness and reproducibility. The soundness scores must be justified by the text of the review.\n\n## Overall Assessment Levels\n\nThe review form includes specific assessment categories with scores:\n\n- 5 = Consider for Award: I think this paper could be considered for an outstanding paper award at an *ACL conference (up to top 2.5% papers).\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle: I think this paper needs substantial revisions that can be completed by the next ARR cycle.\n- 1.5 = Resubmit after next cycle: I think this paper needs substantial revisions that cannot be completed by the next ARR cycle.\n- 1 = Do not resubmit: This paper has to be fully redone, or it is not relevant to the *ACL community (e.g. it is in no way related to computational processing of language).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1446a884-2136-4659-bcf0-843e85ca00ce","uuid":"649d0749-fc0e-40d1-9c73-32905db4581d","timestamp":"2026-05-17T08:59:01.274Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"site:aclrollingreview.org reviewform scoring criteria overall assessment","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Ce6G3MGmsj9iwpA4afDfAD","content":[{"title":"Review Form","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"},{"title":"ARR Reviewer Guidelines","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines"},{"title":"ARR Area Chair Guidelines","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines"},{"title":"Response to ACL 2023 Questions","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/ACL-2023-session/"},{"title":"Action Editor Form","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/actioneditorform"},{"title":"Authors Guidelines","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors"},{"title":"CALL FOR PAPERS – ACL Rolling Review","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/cfp"},{"title":"How ARR works – ACL Rolling Review","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewing"},{"title":"Area Keywords at ARR","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/areas"},{"title":"ARR SAC Guidelines","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/sacguidelines"}]},"I found relevant information about the ACL Rolling Review (ARR) scoring criteria and overall assessment. Here's what the search results show:\n\n## Scoring Criteria\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation.\n\nThe Overall assessment score is an explicit recommendation for the outcome of this paper, if it were committed to an *ACL venue. This is a composite score reflecting your assessment of soundness, excitement, and also other factors like novelty and impact.\n\n## Soundness Criteria\n\nFor main vs Findings papers: the main criteria for Findings are soundness and reproducibility. The soundness scores must be justified by the text of the review.\n\n## Overall Assessment Levels\n\nThe review form includes specific assessment categories with scores:\n\n- 5 = Consider for Award: I think this paper could be considered for an outstanding paper award at an *ACL conference (up to top 2.5% papers).\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle: I think this paper needs substantial revisions that can be completed by the next ARR cycle.\n- 1.5 = Resubmit after next cycle: I think this paper needs substantial revisions that cannot be completed by the next ARR cycle.\n- 1 = Do not resubmit: This paper has to be fully redone, or it is not relevant to the *ACL community (e.g. it is in no way related to computational processing of language)."],"durationSeconds":6.871873856999999}}
