{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01T9fYmfGSGpHZMTwHiHosex","type":"tool_result","content":"# ACL Rolling Review Scoring Scales\n\n## Overall Assessment\n- **5** = \"Consider for Award: I think this paper could be considered for an outstanding paper award\"\n- **4.5** = Borderline Award\n- **4** = \"Conference: I think this paper could be accepted to an *ACL conference\"\n- **3.5** = Borderline Conference\n- **3** = \"Findings: I think this paper could be accepted to the Findings of the ACL\"\n- **2.5** = Borderline Findings\n- **2** = \"Resubmit next cycle: I think this paper needs substantial revisions\"\n- **1.5** = \"Resubmit after next cycle: I think this paper needs substantial revisions that cannot be completed\"\n- **1** = \"Do not resubmit: This paper has to be fully redone\"\n\n## Soundness\n- **5** = \"Excellent: This study is one of the most thorough I have seen\"\n- **4.5** = (intermediate)\n- **4** = \"Strong: This study provides sufficient support for all of its claims\"\n- **3.5** = (intermediate)\n- **3** = \"Acceptable: This study provides sufficient support for its main claims\"\n- **2.5** = (intermediate)\n- **2** = \"Poor: Some of the main claims are not sufficiently supported\"\n- **1.5** = (intermediate)\n- **1** = \"Major Issues: This study is not yet sufficiently thorough to warrant publication\"\n\n## Excitement\n- **5** = \"Highly Exciting: I would recommend this paper to others\"\n- **4.5** = (intermediate)\n- **4** = \"Exciting: I would mention this paper to others\"\n- **3.5** = (intermediate)\n- **3** = \"Interesting: I might mention some points of this paper to others\"\n- **2.5** = (intermediate)\n- **2** = \"Potentially Interesting: this paper does not resonate with me\"\n- **1.5** = (intermediate)\n- **1** = \"Not Exciting: this paper does not resonate with me\"\n\n## Reviewer Confidence\n- **5** = \"Positive that my evaluation is correct. I read the paper very carefully\"\n- **4** = \"Quite sure. I tried to check the important points carefully\"\n- **3** = \"Pretty sure, but there's a chance I missed something\"\n- **2** = \"Willing to defend my evaluation, but it is fairly likely I missed some details\"\n- **1** = \"Not my area, or paper is very hard to understand\"\n\n## Reproducibility\n- **5** = \"They could easily reproduce the results\"\n- **4** = \"They could mostly reproduce the results, but there may be some variation\"\n- **3** = \"They could reproduce the results with some difficulty\"\n- **2** = \"They would be hard pressed to reproduce the results\"\n- **1** = \"They would not be able to reproduce the results\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1446a884-2136-4659-bcf0-843e85ca00ce","uuid":"e406f631-1e51-4354-a669-dea30fbc89e7","timestamp":"2026-05-17T08:59:17.518Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":25365,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# ACL Rolling Review Scoring Scales\n\n## Overall Assessment\n- **5** = \"Consider for Award: I think this paper could be considered for an outstanding paper award\"\n- **4.5** = Borderline Award\n- **4** = \"Conference: I think this paper could be accepted to an *ACL conference\"\n- **3.5** = Borderline Conference\n- **3** = \"Findings: I think this paper could be accepted to the Findings of the ACL\"\n- **2.5** = Borderline Findings\n- **2** = \"Resubmit next cycle: I think this paper needs substantial revisions\"\n- **1.5** = \"Resubmit after next cycle: I think this paper needs substantial revisions that cannot be completed\"\n- **1** = \"Do not resubmit: This paper has to be fully redone\"\n\n## Soundness\n- **5** = \"Excellent: This study is one of the most thorough I have seen\"\n- **4.5** = (intermediate)\n- **4** = \"Strong: This study provides sufficient support for all of its claims\"\n- **3.5** = (intermediate)\n- **3** = \"Acceptable: This study provides sufficient support for its main claims\"\n- **2.5** = (intermediate)\n- **2** = \"Poor: Some of the main claims are not sufficiently supported\"\n- **1.5** = (intermediate)\n- **1** = \"Major Issues: This study is not yet sufficiently thorough to warrant publication\"\n\n## Excitement\n- **5** = \"Highly Exciting: I would recommend this paper to others\"\n- **4.5** = (intermediate)\n- **4** = \"Exciting: I would mention this paper to others\"\n- **3.5** = (intermediate)\n- **3** = \"Interesting: I might mention some points of this paper to others\"\n- **2.5** = (intermediate)\n- **2** = \"Potentially Interesting: this paper does not resonate with me\"\n- **1.5** = (intermediate)\n- **1** = \"Not Exciting: this paper does not resonate with me\"\n\n## Reviewer Confidence\n- **5** = \"Positive that my evaluation is correct. I read the paper very carefully\"\n- **4** = \"Quite sure. I tried to check the important points carefully\"\n- **3** = \"Pretty sure, but there's a chance I missed something\"\n- **2** = \"Willing to defend my evaluation, but it is fairly likely I missed some details\"\n- **1** = \"Not my area, or paper is very hard to understand\"\n\n## Reproducibility\n- **5** = \"They could easily reproduce the results\"\n- **4** = \"They could mostly reproduce the results, but there may be some variation\"\n- **3** = \"They could reproduce the results with some difficulty\"\n- **2** = \"They would be hard pressed to reproduce the results\"\n- **1** = \"They would not be able to reproduce the results\"","durationMs":6363,"url":"https://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017Y9NW4v7N73sYJXG7y5yEk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have:\n1. The full paper content (11 pages)\n2. The review context YAML with imagined experiments and expected results\n3. The ARR/EMNLP scoring scale\n\nNow I need to dispatch the full review with 2 Reviewers following the dream-review-full skill dispatch template:\n- R1: Method + Experiments\n- R2: Novelty + Related Work\n- Then a full roundtable: Step 1 independent → Step 2 cross-sharing → Step 3 convergence → Step 4 synthesis\n\nLet me now launch two reviewer agents in parallel. Each reviewer should independently review the paper without knowing what the other thinks.\n\n\nI'll structure this carefully by dispatching R1 and R2 as parallel agents, then synthesizing the roundtable myself. For context, the paper proposes a feature-based approach using 19 statistical features from a reference model to estimate how many recursive fine-tuning cycles a text has undergone, testing this on Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-1B, and GPT-2 XL across depths 0-3 using random forest and logistic regression classifiers.\n\nThe core results show 70-85% accuracy under controlled conditions, with an interesting degradation atlas revealing how the signal breaks down—it drops to 55% at 14B parameters, 50% with prompted generation, and collapses to 23-30% false positive rates on web-crawled data. The cross-strategy pipeline also fails, achieving only 57.5% strategy classification accuracy. Looking at the review context, this is flagged as a lower-tier paper, and I can see which experiments made it into the final submission: the 13B validation, wild-data calibration, and prompted generation tests all appear, while the DCOL end-to-end learning approach was abandoned after failing.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":830,"cache_read_input_tokens":55879,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":830},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1446a884-2136-4659-bcf0-843e85ca00ce","uuid":"453b80f6-a211-484e-87c6-48e85375ef95"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017Y9NW4v7N73sYJXG7y5yEk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have the paper, review context, and ARR/EMNLP scoring scale. Now dispatching two independent reviewers in parallel."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":830,"cache_read_input_tokens":55879,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":830},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1446a884-2136-4659-bcf0-843e85ca00ce","uuid":"b417da06-752a-412e-b7b8-539c2b1f6669"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017Y9NW4v7N73sYJXG7y5yEk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XYg221WCdZbKMQzBV1heER","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Method + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 Main conference submission. Your focus areas are **methodology soundness** and **experimental rigor**.\n\n## ARR Scoring Scale\n- Overall: 1 (do not resubmit) to 5 (award-worthy). 4 = accept to conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = resubmit.\n- Soundness: 1 (major issues) to 5 (excellent). 4 = strong, 3 = acceptable, 2 = poor.\n- Excitement: 1 (not exciting) to 5 (highly exciting). 4 = exciting, 3 = interesting.\n- Confidence: 1-5. Reproducibility: 1-5.\n- Half-point increments allowed.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Characterizing Recursive Generation Depth: Feature-Based Estimation, Degradation Atlas, and Boundary Conditions\"\n\n**Core task**: Ordinal generation depth estimation — predicting how many recursive fine-tuning and re-generation cycles (depth d ∈ {0,1,2,3}) text has undergone, from text alone.\n\n**Method**: \n- Recursive generation protocol: 3 base models (Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-1B, GPT-2 XL) × 3 decoding strategies (nucleus p=0.95, temp τ=0.9, top-k k=50) × 4 depths. BOS-seeded unconditional generation, 498-token passages, 1000 passages per condition. LoRA fine-tuning (rank 16, α=32) at each depth.\n- Feature extraction: 19 statistical features from Pythia-160M reference model perplexity/surprisal (6 perplexity stats, 5 surprisal stats, 4 lexical diversity, 4 distributional shape).\n- Classification: RF (500 trees, max depth 12) and L2-regularized LR, 5-fold CV within each model-strategy combo.\n- EDD metric: Effective Discriminable Depth at threshold T = max k where all adjacent AUC ≥ T.\n\n**Key results**:\n- Controlled settings (within-strategy, BOS-seeded, 1-1.5B): 70-85% 4-class accuracy. RF consistently outperforms LR.\n- Atlas findings: (i) perplexity tail stats (p90_ppl, var_surprisal) universally dominate feature importance; (ii) EDD=3 at T=0.75 for most configs, drops at T=0.80; (iii) temperature sampling causes feature-boundary reversal at d1v2 interface (cross-strategy LOO d1v2 AUC drops to 0.13 for GPT-2 XL).\n- Boundary conditions: 14B scale → 55% accuracy, EDD=1; prompted generation → 50% accuracy, EDD=1; web-crawl domain → 23-30% FPR.\n- DCOL (contrastive ordinal learning): 6 variants all failed, best = 64.5% (RoBERTa-large), well below RF 78.2%.\n- Cross-strategy pipeline: strategy classifier only 57.5%, predicted two-stage (70.6%) < naive pooled (72.0%).\n\n**Tables**: 10 tables total. Table 1 (main results), Table 2 (EDD at thresholds), Table 3 (scale comparison), Table 4 (feature importance shift at 14B), Table 5 (prompted vs BOS), Table 6 (per-class recall prompted), Table 7 (fine-tuning loss), Table 8 (wild-data FPR), Table 9 (boundary summary), Table 10 (cross-strategy pipeline).\n\n**4 contributions claimed**: (1) task formalization + benchmark, (2) Depth Degradation Atlas (primary), (3) boundary condition characterization, (4) negative result on end-to-end learning.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe full imagined experiment list includes:\n1. DCOL component ablation — cancelled because DCOL v6 failed (64.5% vs RF 78.2%)\n2. 13B clean FP16 scale validation (Qwen2.5-14B) — expected 55% 4-class accuracy, EDD=1 at T=0.75. Mechanism: Pythia-160M reference model perplexity landscape compresses depth gradient of 14B outputs due to cross-scale vocabulary mismatch (151K vs 50K tokens) and smaller per-depth distributional shifts with LoRA on 14B.\n3. Wild-data calibration on RedPajama/Dolma — expected 23-30% FPR on web-crawl, cross-model FPR range 18pp, Brier score 0.28. OLMo lower FPR due to domain match.\n4. Prompted generation ecological validity (GPT-2 XL, topic-seeded) — expected 50% 4-class, EDD=1, 58.6% retention. Prompt anchoring effect compresses per-depth distributional shift.\n5. Cross-strategy two-stage pipeline — expected strategy clf 57.5%, predicted two-stage 70.6% < naive pooled 72.0%. Strategy features are structurally unsuitable for the 19 perplexity features.\n\nThe paper covers experiments 2-5 (experiment 1 was cancelled). Assess whether the paper's presentation of these experiments is complete and rigorous.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (3-4 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, with evidence)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, with evidence, distinguishing major vs minor)\n4. **Methodology critique**: Is the experimental protocol sound? Are there confounds? Is the feature set justified? Is the evaluation (5-fold CV within each config) appropriate?\n5. **Missing experiments or analyses**: What's conspicuously absent? Consider: (a) statistical significance testing, (b) confidence intervals, (c) other reference models besides Pythia-160M, (d) comparison with existing AI-text detectors adapted to ordinal depth, (e) whether 1000 passages per condition is sufficient.\n6. **Reproducibility assessment**\n7. **Questions for authors** (up to 5)\n8. **Scores**: Overall, Soundness, Excitement, Confidence, Reproducibility (use ARR scale with half-points)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Distinguish between fundamental flaws and fixable issues."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":830,"cache_read_input_tokens":55879,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":830},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1446a884-2136-4659-bcf0-843e85ca00ce","uuid":"6385ebda-94f5-4996-85b5-2c0a15bbdc88"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"ac8d854da0ad6578d","tool_use_id":"toolu_01XYg221WCdZbKMQzBV1heER","description":"R1: Method + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 Main conference submission. Your focus areas are **methodology soundness** and **experimental rigor**.\n\n## ARR Scoring Scale\n- Overall: 1 (do not resubmit) to 5 (award-worthy). 4 = accept to conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = resubmit.\n- Soundness: 1 (major issues) to 5 (excellent). 4 = strong, 3 = acceptable, 2 = poor.\n- Excitement: 1 (not exciting) to 5 (highly exciting). 4 = exciting, 3 = interesting.\n- Confidence: 1-5. Reproducibility: 1-5.\n- Half-point increments allowed.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Characterizing Recursive Generation Depth: Feature-Based Estimation, Degradation Atlas, and Boundary Conditions\"\n\n**Core task**: Ordinal generation depth estimation — predicting how many recursive fine-tuning and re-generation cycles (depth d ∈ {0,1,2,3}) text has undergone, from text alone.\n\n**Method**: \n- Recursive generation protocol: 3 base models (Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-1B, GPT-2 XL) × 3 decoding strategies (nucleus p=0.95, temp τ=0.9, top-k k=50) × 4 depths. BOS-seeded unconditional generation, 498-token passages, 1000 passages per condition. LoRA fine-tuning (rank 16, α=32) at each depth.\n- Feature extraction: 19 statistical features from Pythia-160M reference model perplexity/surprisal (6 perplexity stats, 5 surprisal stats, 4 lexical diversity, 4 distributional shape).\n- Classification: RF (500 trees, max depth 12) and L2-regularized LR, 5-fold CV within each model-strategy combo.\n- EDD metric: Effective Discriminable Depth at threshold T = max k where all adjacent AUC ≥ T.\n\n**Key results**:\n- Controlled settings (within-strategy, BOS-seeded, 1-1.5B): 70-85% 4-class accuracy. RF consistently outperforms LR.\n- Atlas findings: (i) perplexity tail stats (p90_ppl, var_surprisal) universally dominate feature importance; (ii) EDD=3 at T=0.75 for most configs, drops at T=0.80; (iii) temperature sampling causes feature-boundary reversal at d1v2 interface (cross-strategy LOO d1v2 AUC drops to 0.13 for GPT-2 XL).\n- Boundary conditions: 14B scale → 55% accuracy, EDD=1; prompted generation → 50% accuracy, EDD=1; web-crawl domain → 23-30% FPR.\n- DCOL (contrastive ordinal learning): 6 variants all failed, best = 64.5% (RoBERTa-large), well below RF 78.2%.\n- Cross-strategy pipeline: strategy classifier only 57.5%, predicted two-stage (70.6%) < naive pooled (72.0%).\n\n**Tables**: 10 tables total. Table 1 (main results), Table 2 (EDD at thresholds), Table 3 (scale comparison), Table 4 (feature importance shift at 14B), Table 5 (prompted vs BOS), Table 6 (per-class recall prompted), Table 7 (fine-tuning loss), Table 8 (wild-data FPR), Table 9 (boundary summary), Table 10 (cross-strategy pipeline).\n\n**4 contributions claimed**: (1) task formalization + benchmark, (2) Depth Degradation Atlas (primary), (3) boundary condition characterization, (4) negative result on end-to-end learning.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe full imagined experiment list includes:\n1. DCOL component ablation — cancelled because DCOL v6 failed (64.5% vs RF 78.2%)\n2. 13B clean FP16 scale validation (Qwen2.5-14B) — expected 55% 4-class accuracy, EDD=1 at T=0.75. Mechanism: Pythia-160M reference model perplexity landscape compresses depth gradient of 14B outputs due to cross-scale vocabulary mismatch (151K vs 50K tokens) and smaller per-depth distributional shifts with LoRA on 14B.\n3. Wild-data calibration on RedPajama/Dolma — expected 23-30% FPR on web-crawl, cross-model FPR range 18pp, Brier score 0.28. OLMo lower FPR due to domain match.\n4. Prompted generation ecological validity (GPT-2 XL, topic-seeded) — expected 50% 4-class, EDD=1, 58.6% retention. Prompt anchoring effect compresses per-depth distributional shift.\n5. Cross-strategy two-stage pipeline — expected strategy clf 57.5%, predicted two-stage 70.6% < naive pooled 72.0%. Strategy features are structurally unsuitable for the 19 perplexity features.\n\nThe paper covers experiments 2-5 (experiment 1 was cancelled). Assess whether the paper's presentation of these experiments is complete and rigorous.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (3-4 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, with evidence)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, with evidence, distinguishing major vs minor)\n4. **Methodology critique**: Is the experimental protocol sound? Are there confounds? Is the feature set justified? Is the evaluation (5-fold CV within each config) appropriate?\n5. **Missing experiments or analyses**: What's conspicuously absent? Consider: (a) statistical significance testing, (b) confidence intervals, (c) other reference models besides Pythia-160M, (d) comparison with existing AI-text detectors adapted to ordinal depth, (e) whether 1000 passages per condition is sufficient.\n6. **Reproducibility assessment**\n7. **Questions for authors** (up to 5)\n8. **Scores**: Overall, Soundness, Excitement, Confidence, Reproducibility (use ARR scale with half-points)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Distinguish between fundamental flaws and fixable issues.","uuid":"db50758c-2796-49ef-85aa-d991489435d8","session_id":"1446a884-2136-4659-bcf0-843e85ca00ce"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 Main conference submission. Your focus areas are **methodology soundness** and **experimental rigor**.\n\n## ARR Scoring Scale\n- Overall: 1 (do not resubmit) to 5 (award-worthy). 4 = accept to conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = resubmit.\n- Soundness: 1 (major issues) to 5 (excellent). 4 = strong, 3 = acceptable, 2 = poor.\n- Excitement: 1 (not exciting) to 5 (highly exciting). 4 = exciting, 3 = interesting.\n- Confidence: 1-5. Reproducibility: 1-5.\n- Half-point increments allowed.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Characterizing Recursive Generation Depth: Feature-Based Estimation, Degradation Atlas, and Boundary Conditions\"\n\n**Core task**: Ordinal generation depth estimation — predicting how many recursive fine-tuning and re-generation cycles (depth d ∈ {0,1,2,3}) text has undergone, from text alone.\n\n**Method**: \n- Recursive generation protocol: 3 base models (Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-1B, GPT-2 XL) × 3 decoding strategies (nucleus p=0.95, temp τ=0.9, top-k k=50) × 4 depths. BOS-seeded unconditional generation, 498-token passages, 1000 passages per condition. LoRA fine-tuning (rank 16, α=32) at each depth.\n- Feature extraction: 19 statistical features from Pythia-160M reference model perplexity/surprisal (6 perplexity stats, 5 surprisal stats, 4 lexical diversity, 4 distributional shape).\n- Classification: RF (500 trees, max depth 12) and L2-regularized LR, 5-fold CV within each model-strategy combo.\n- EDD metric: Effective Discriminable Depth at threshold T = max k where all adjacent AUC ≥ T.\n\n**Key results**:\n- Controlled settings (within-strategy, BOS-seeded, 1-1.5B): 70-85% 4-class accuracy. RF consistently outperforms LR.\n- Atlas findings: (i) perplexity tail stats (p90_ppl, var_surprisal) universally dominate feature importance; (ii) EDD=3 at T=0.75 for most configs, drops at T=0.80; (iii) temperature sampling causes feature-boundary reversal at d1v2 interface (cross-strategy LOO d1v2 AUC drops to 0.13 for GPT-2 XL).\n- Boundary conditions: 14B scale → 55% accuracy, EDD=1; prompted generation → 50% accuracy, EDD=1; web-crawl domain → 23-30% FPR.\n- DCOL (contrastive ordinal learning): 6 variants all failed, best = 64.5% (RoBERTa-large), well below RF 78.2%.\n- Cross-strategy pipeline: strategy classifier only 57.5%, predicted two-stage (70.6%) < naive pooled (72.0%).\n\n**Tables**: 10 tables total. Table 1 (main results), Table 2 (EDD at thresholds), Table 3 (scale comparison), Table 4 (feature importance shift at 14B), Table 5 (prompted vs BOS), Table 6 (per-class recall prompted), Table 7 (fine-tuning loss), Table 8 (wild-data FPR), Table 9 (boundary summary), Table 10 (cross-strategy pipeline).\n\n**4 contributions claimed**: (1) task formalization + benchmark, (2) Depth Degradation Atlas (primary), (3) boundary condition characterization, (4) negative result on end-to-end learning.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe full imagined experiment list includes:\n1. DCOL component ablation — cancelled because DCOL v6 failed (64.5% vs RF 78.2%)\n2. 13B clean FP16 scale validation (Qwen2.5-14B) — expected 55% 4-class accuracy, EDD=1 at T=0.75. Mechanism: Pythia-160M reference model perplexity landscape compresses depth gradient of 14B outputs due to cross-scale vocabulary mismatch (151K vs 50K tokens) and smaller per-depth distributional shifts with LoRA on 14B.\n3. Wild-data calibration on RedPajama/Dolma — expected 23-30% FPR on web-crawl, cross-model FPR range 18pp, Brier score 0.28. OLMo lower FPR due to domain match.\n4. Prompted generation ecological validity (GPT-2 XL, topic-seeded) — expected 50% 4-class, EDD=1, 58.6% retention. Prompt anchoring effect compresses per-depth distributional shift.\n5. Cross-strategy two-stage pipeline — expected strategy clf 57.5%, predicted two-stage 70.6% < naive pooled 72.0%. Strategy features are structurally unsuitable for the 19 perplexity features.\n\nThe paper covers experiments 2-5 (experiment 1 was cancelled). Assess whether the paper's presentation of these experiments is complete and rigorous.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (3-4 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, with evidence)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, with evidence, distinguishing major vs minor)\n4. **Methodology critique**: Is the experimental protocol sound? Are there confounds? Is the feature set justified? Is the evaluation (5-fold CV within each config) appropriate?\n5. **Missing experiments or analyses**: What's conspicuously absent? Consider: (a) statistical significance testing, (b) confidence intervals, (c) other reference models besides Pythia-160M, (d) comparison with existing AI-text detectors adapted to ordinal depth, (e) whether 1000 passages per condition is sufficient.\n6. **Reproducibility assessment**\n7. **Questions for authors** (up to 5)\n8. **Scores**: Overall, Soundness, Excitement, Confidence, Reproducibility (use ARR scale with half-points)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Distinguish between fundamental flaws and fixable issues."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XYg221WCdZbKMQzBV1heER","session_id":"1446a884-2136-4659-bcf0-843e85ca00ce","uuid":"d16ffa45-d73f-4731-82a4-b9ef7bc19cdf","timestamp":"2026-05-17T09:00:21.657Z"}
