{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KSezwS8u2Xph2pMdbQw3Sp","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513_2/projects/recursive_gen_depth_est/literature_map.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Literature Map — recursive_gen_depth_est\\\\n# Last updated: 2026-05-15\\\\n# Purpose: Systematic reference tracking for EMNLP ARR May 25 submission\\\\n# Total entries: 43 (35 original + 8 R2-N2 supplement)\\\\n#\\\\n# Tags used for cite_in: introduction, related-work, background, method,\\\\n#   experiments, discussion, limitations, dcol-section\\\\n\\\\n# =============================================================\\\\n# MODEL COLLAPSE — foundational theory & recursive generation\\\\n# =============================================================\\\\n\\\\nshumailov2024_nature:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"AI models collapse when trained on recursively generated data\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Shumailov, Shumaylov, Zhao, Papernot, Anderson, Gal]\\\\n  venue: Nature\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  doi: \\\\\\\"10.1038/s41586-024-07566-y\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [model-collapse, recursive-generation, tail-erosion]\\\\n  relevance: direct\\\\n  cite_in: [introduction, related-work, background]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Foundational model collapse paper. Tail erosion across recursive generations.\\\\n    Perplexity monotonically increases over 40 generations. Does NOT compare\\\\n    sampling strategies—uses standard sampling (T=1).\\\\n\\\\nmarchi2024_heat_death:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Heat Death of Generative Models in Closed-Loop Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Marchi, Soatto, Chaudhari, Tabuada]\\\\n  venue: ICASSP 2025\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2404.02325\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [temperature, convergence, dynamical-systems, mode-collapse]\\\\n  relevance: direct\\\\n  cite_in: [discussion]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Proves any T!=1 causes asymptotic degeneration. T<1 amplifies probability\\\\n    differences (mode collapse), T>1 contracts toward uniform. Key theoretical\\\\n    support for our tau=0.9 accelerated convergence finding.\\\\n\\\\ndohmatob2024_tale_of_tails:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"A Tale of Tails: Model Collapse as a Change of Scaling Laws\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Dohmatob, Feng, Yang, Charton, Kempe]\\\\n  venue: ICML 2024\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2402.07043\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [scaling-laws, tail-narrowing, tail-cutting, temperature, top-p, top-k]\\\\n  relevance: direct\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work, discussion]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Distinguishes tail narrowing (temperature: q_i^T ~ i^{-beta/T}) from\\\\n    tail cutting (top-p/top-k: hard truncation). Different degradation patterns.\\\\n    Explains why temp produces different statistical fingerprint from truncation.\\\\n\\\\ndohmatob2025_strong_collapse:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Strong Model Collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Dohmatob, Feng, Subramonian, Kempe]\\\\n  venue: ICLR 2025 Spotlight\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2410.04840\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [model-collapse, scaling, synthetic-data]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Even 0.1% synthetic data fraction causes collapse. Larger models amplify collapse.\\\\n    Bias-variance decomposition via random projections.\\\\n\\\\ndohmatob2024_demystified:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Model Collapse Demystified: The Case of Regression\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Dohmatob, Feng, Kempe]\\\\n  venue: NeurIPS 2024\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2402.07712\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [model-collapse, regression, random-matrix-theory]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Test error grows linearly with iteration count in regression.\\\\n    Supports our finding of monotonically evolving features across depths.\\\\n\\\\nalemohammad2024_mad:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Self-Consuming Generative Models Go MAD\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Alemohammad, Casco-Rodriguez, et al.]\\\\n  venue: ICLR 2024\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2307.01850\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [autophagous, sampling-bias, precision-recall, MAD]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [introduction, related-work]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Biased sampling keeps precision but kills recall. Unbiased causes mode drift.\\\\n    tau=0.9 maps to their biased sampling regime.\\\\n\\\\ngerstgrasser2024_accumulate:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Is Model Collapse Inevitable? Breaking the Curse of Recursion by Accumulating Real and Synthetic Data\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Gerstgrasser, Schaeffer, et al.]\\\\n  venue: arXiv preprint\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2404.01413\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [model-collapse, data-accumulation, mitigation]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work, discussion]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Data accumulation prevents collapse (bounded test error).\\\\n    Important caveat: in accumulation regime, depth signal may be weaker.\\\\n\\\\ndrayson2025_detection_prevents_collapse:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Machine-generated text detection prevents language model collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Drayson et al.]\\\\n  venue: EMNLP 2025 Findings\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2502.15654\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [temperature, diversity, recursive-training, detection]\\\\n  relevance: direct\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Temperature tau=0.9 produces most repetitive outputs; diversity drops ~70%\\\\n    over 99 recursive generations (GPT-2, SmolLM2). Perplexity degradation more\\\\n    moderate than deterministic methods. Detection as filter vs our depth estimation.\\\\n\\\\nbakshi2026_collapse_improvement:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"From Collapse to Improvement: Statistical Perspectives on Iterative Training\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Bakshi, Chakraborty]\\\\n  venue: arXiv preprint\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2602.10531\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [model-collapse, non-monotonic, data-mixing]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [discussion]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Iterative training with fresh real data can *improve* performance.\\\\n    Non-monotonic dynamics complicate simple depth-signal assumptions.\\\\n\\\\nwang2026_contamination:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Can Generative AI Survive Data Contamination? Theoretical Guarantees\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Wang, Niu, Li]\\\\n  venue: arXiv preprint\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2602.16065\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [model-collapse, contamination, theoretical]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Extends collapse theory beyond Gaussian/discrete to realistic distributions.\\\\n    Provides theoretical grounding for depth signal existence.\\\\n\\\\nfalahati2026_curated:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Curated Synthetic Data Doesn't Have to Collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Falahati, Amiri, Larson, Golab]\\\\n  venue: arXiv preprint\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2605.07724\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [curation, collapse-prevention, pluralistic]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [discussion, limitations]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Pluralistic curation prevents collapse. Harder case for our method —\\\\n    curated pipelines may produce depth-invariant text.\\\\n\\\\nknowledge_collapse2025:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Knowledge Collapse in LLMs: Fluency Survives but Facts Fail\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Keisha, Wu, Wang, Koshiyama, Treleaven]\\\\n  venue: arXiv preprint\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2509.04796\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [knowledge-collapse, fluency, factual-degradation]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [discussion]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Three-stage collapse: factual accuracy degrades while fluency persists.\\\\n    Surface perplexity features still capture depth signal despite fluency preservation.\\\\n\\\\nsynthetic_eggs2026:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Synthetic Eggs in Many Baskets: Impact of Synthetic Data Diversity on LLM Fine-Tuning\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Schaffelder, Gatt]\\\\n  venue: ACL 2026 Findings\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2511.01490\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [diversity, collapse-mitigation, Self-BLEU]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Multi-source synthetic data reduces collapse. Their diversity metrics\\\\n    (Self-BLEU, lexical diversity) are candidate depth features.\\\\n\\\\nmohamed2025_broken_telephone:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"LLM as a Broken Telephone: Iterative Generation Distorts Information\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Mohamed, Geng, Vazirgiannis, Shang]\\\\n  venue: ACL 2025\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2502.20258\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [iterative-generation, information-distortion, translation-chain]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [discussion]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Iterative generation (translation/paraphrase chains) produces monotonically\\\\n    increasing information distortion. Cross-task evidence that iterated signals\\\\n    accumulate — supports our depth signal persistence finding.\\\\n\\\\nsigma2026_spectral:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"SIGMA: Scalable Spectral Insights for LLM Model Collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [\\\\\\\"(multiple)\\\\\\\"]\\\\n  venue: arXiv preprint\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2601.03385\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [spectral-analysis, gram-matrix, representation-collapse, 50-generations]\\\\n  relevance: direct\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work, discussion]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Gram matrix spectral analysis tracks representation space contraction over\\\\n    50 recursive generations. Proposes Sigma-UB metric. Complementary to our\\\\n    EDD — they measure geometric collapse, we measure statistical distinguishability.\\\\n    Tests 50 generations but does NOT do inter-generation classification.\\\\n\\\\n# --- R2-N2 supplement: model collapse empirical ---\\\\n\\\\nshi2025_closer_look:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"A Closer Look at Model Collapse: From a Generalization-to-Memorization Perspective\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Shi, Wu, Zhang, Zhang, Tao, Qu]\\\\n  venue: NeurIPS 2025 (Spotlight)\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2509.16499\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [model-collapse, entropy, memorization, intrinsic-dimension]\\\\n  relevance: direct\\\\n  role: background\\\\n  verified: true\\\\n  integration: \\\\\\\"整合，entropy ↔ depth signal 支撑特征选择论证\\\\\\\"\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work, discussion]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Identifies generalization-to-memorization transition during recursive training.\\\\n    Entropy of training data decreases over iterations, strongly correlated with\\\\n    model degradation. Entropy decline IS a measurable depth signal — directly\\\\n    supports our statistical feature approach to depth estimation.\\\\n\\\\nyi2026_escaping_collapse:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Escaping Model Collapse via Synthetic Data Verification: Near-term Improvements and Long-term Convergence\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Yi, Liu, Cheng, Xu]\\\\n  venue: 3rd DATA-FM Workshop @ ICLR 2026\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2510.16657\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [model-collapse, verification, synthetic-data, mitigation]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  role: background\\\\n  verified: true\\\\n  integration: \\\\\\\"引用但降级——related work 提一句，不支撑核心 claim（workshop paper）\\\\\\\"\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Verifier-guided retraining prevents collapse. Our depth estimation\\\\n    can serve AS the verifier — depth score enables graduated filtering\\\\n    rather than binary accept/reject. NOTE: workshop paper, not main conference.\\\\n\\\\n# =============================================================\\\\n# DECODING STRATEGIES\\\\n# =============================================================\\\\n\\\\nholtzman2020_nucleus:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"The Curious Case of Neural Text Degeneration\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Holtzman, Buys, Du, Forbes, Choi]\\\\n  venue: ICLR 2020\\\\n  year: 2020\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"1904.09751\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [nucleus-sampling, temperature, text-degeneration, unreliable-tail]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [background, method]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Proposes nucleus sampling. Temperature+top-k leads to repetition.\\\\n    Key concept: unreliable tail of low-probability tokens.\\\\n\\\\nfinlayson2024_closing:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Closing the Curious Case of Neural Text Degeneration\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Finlayson, Hewitt, Koller, Swayamdipta, Sabharwal]\\\\n  venue: ICLR 2024\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2310.01693\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [softmax-bottleneck, truncation-sampling, precise-sampling]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Truncation sampling eliminates softmax bottleneck errors. Mechanistic\\\\n    difference between truncation and scaling approaches.\\\\n\\\\ngarces_arias2025_decoding_decoded:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Decoding Decoded: Understanding Hyperparameter Effects in Open-Ended Text Generation\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Garces Arias et al.]\\\\n  venue: COLING 2025\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  tags: [decoding, hyperparameters, model-dependent]\\\\n  relevance: direct\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work, discussion]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Confirms optimal decoding configuration is model-dependent.\\\\n    Directly supports D006 finding (strategy effect is model-dependent).\\\\n    Key external validation for our interaction effects claim.\\\\n\\\\nwiher2022_decoding:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"On Decoding Strategies for Neural Text Generators\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Wiher, Meister, Cotterell]\\\\n  venue: TACL 2022\\\\n  year: 2022\\\\n  tags: [decoding, task-dependent, evaluation]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Decoding strategy effects are task-dependent.\\\\n    Supports our finding that strategy effects on depth signal vary by model.\\\\n\\\\nbasu2021_mirostat:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Mirostat: A Neural Text Decoding Algorithm that Directly Controls Perplexity\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Basu, Sachdev, Kamdar, Nishi]\\\\n  venue: ICLR 2021\\\\n  year: 2021\\\\n  tags: [perplexity-control, sampling, cross-entropy]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work, discussion]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Perplexity and sampling parameters have non-linear relationship.\\\\n    Relevant to p90_ppl capturing depth signal better than mean_ppl\\\\n    under heavy-tailed distributions.\\\\n\\\\n# =============================================================\\\\n# BINARY / ZERO-SHOT DETECTION\\\\n# =============================================================\\\\n\\\\nmitchell2023_detectgpt:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"DetectGPT: Zero-Shot Machine-Generated Text Detection using Probability Curvature\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Mitchell, Lee, Khazatsky, Manning, Finn]\\\\n  venue: ICML 2023\\\\n  year: 2023\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2301.11305\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [zero-shot, detection, curvature, perturbation, binary]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Foundational zero-shot detector using log-probability curvature via\\\\n    random perturbations. Binary only. Fast-DetectGPT improves efficiency.\\\\n\\\\nsadasivan2023_paraphrase:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Can AI-Generated Text be Reliably Detected?\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Sadasivan, Kumar, Balasubramanian, Wang, Feizi]\\\\n  venue: arXiv preprint\\\\n  year: 2023\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2303.11156\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [detection-limits, paraphrase-attack, impossibility]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work, limitations]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Shows paraphrase attacks evade all detectors; proves impossibility\\\\n    result when model and human distributions overlap sufficiently.\\\\n    Important limitation context for our depth estimation approach.\\\\n\\\\nbinoculars2024:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Spotting LLMs With Binoculars: Zero-Shot Detection of Machine-Generated Text\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Hans, Schwarzschild, Manber, et al.]\\\\n  venue: ICML 2024\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2401.12070\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [zero-shot, detection, perplexity-ratio, binary]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Two-model perplexity ratio for zero-shot binary detection.\\\\n    We extend beyond binary to ordinal depth.\\\\n\\\\nfast_detectgpt2024:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Fast-DetectGPT: Efficient Zero-Shot Detection of Machine-Generated Text via Conditional Probability Curvature\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Bao et al.]\\\\n  venue: ICLR 2024\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2310.05130\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [zero-shot, detection, curvature, binary]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Conditional probability curvature for efficient binary detection.\\\\n    Cannot distinguish depth levels.\\\\n\\\\ndiveye2026:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"DivEye: Diversity Boosts AI-Generated Text Detection\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Basani, Chen]\\\\n  venue: ICLR 2026 / TMLR\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2509.18880\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [surprisal-diversity, statistical-features, detection]\\\\n  relevance: direct\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work, method]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Token-level surprisal diversity features for binary detection.\\\\n    Feature overlap with our pipeline. We extend similar features\\\\n    to ordinal depth regression.\\\\n\\\\nloki2025:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"LOKI: A Comprehensive Synthetic Data Detection Benchmark using Large Multimodal Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Ye, Zhou, Huang, Zhang, Bai, Kang, He, Lin, Wang, Wu, Wu, Chen, Lin, He, Li]\\\\n  venue: ICLR 2025 Spotlight\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2410.09732\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [benchmark, multimodal, detection]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Multimodal synthetic data detection benchmark. Binary only, no depth.\\\\n\\\\ngehrmann2019_gltr:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"GLTR: Statistical Detection and Visualization of Generated Text\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Gehrmann, Strobelt, Rush]\\\\n  venue: ACL 2019\\\\n  year: 2019\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"1906.04043\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [detection, statistical-features, perplexity, rank, entropy]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Foundational statistical detection using log-likelihood, rank, entropy.\\\\n    Our feature system extends this paradigm to recursive depth estimation.\\\\n\\\\npudasaini2026_detection_fails:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Why AI-Generated Text Detection Fails: Evidence from Explainable AI\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Pudasaini et al.]\\\\n  venue: arXiv preprint\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2603.23146\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [detection-failure, distribution-shift, fragility]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [limitations]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Detection features degrade under distribution shift.\\\\n    If binary features are domain-fragile, ordinal depth features may be too.\\\\n\\\\n# --- R2-N2 supplement: detection methods ---\\\\n\\\\ntulchinskii2024_intrinsic_dim:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Intrinsic Dimension Estimation for Robust Detection of AI-Generated Texts\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Tulchinskii, Kuznetsov, et al.]\\\\n  venue: NeurIPS 2023\\\\n  year: 2023\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2306.04723\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [intrinsic-dimension, geometric-features, detection, cross-domain]\\\\n  relevance: direct\\\\n  role: method-component\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work, method]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Intrinsic dimension of text embeddings ~9 for human, ~7.5 for AI-generated.\\\\n    Geometric invariant enables cross-domain, cross-model robust detection.\\\\n    Complementary feature family to our statistical perplexity features —\\\\n    intrinsic dimension could serve as additional depth feature.\\\\n\\\\nverma2024_ghostbuster:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Ghostbuster: Detecting Text Ghostwritten by Large Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Verma, Fleisig, Tomlin, Klein]\\\\n  venue: NAACL 2024\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2305.15047\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [detection, structured-search, statistical-features, cross-domain]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  role: direct-competitor\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Structured search over combinations of weaker LM features for detection.\\\\n    99.0 F1 cross-domain. Binary only. Feature engineering approach parallels\\\\n    our multi-feature pipeline but targets binary classification, not ordinal depth.\\\\n\\\\nli2024_mage:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"MAGE: Machine-generated Text Detection in the Wild\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Li, Li, et al.]\\\\n  venue: ACL 2024\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2305.13242\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [benchmark, cross-domain, cross-model, detection]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  role: evaluation\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Cross-domain cross-model detection benchmark. Finds significant OOD\\\\n    performance degradation — same generalization challenge our cross-model\\\\n    depth estimation faces. Binary detection only.\\\\n\\\\nwu2025_detection_survey:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"A Survey on LLM-Generated Text Detection: Necessity, Methods, and Future Directions\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Wu, Yang, Zhan, Yuan, Chao, Wong]\\\\n  venue: Computational Linguistics, Vol. 51, Issue 1\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2310.14724\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [survey, detection-taxonomy, watermarking, statistical, neural]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  role: background\\\\n  verified: true\\\\n  integration: \\\\\\\"整合，survey 定位\\\\\\\"\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Comprehensive survey of detection paradigms: watermarking, statistical,\\\\n    neural, human-assisted. Positions our work as extending the statistical\\\\n    paradigm from binary detection to ordinal depth estimation.\\\\n\\\\n# =============================================================\\\\n# ADJACENT TASK — editing extent, attribution\\\\n# =============================================================\\\\n\\\\neditlens2026:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"EditLens: Quantifying the Extent of AI Editing in Text\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Thai et al.]\\\\n  venue: ICLR 2026\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2510.03154\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [editing-extent, continuous-score, mixed-authorship]\\\\n  relevance: direct\\\\n  cite_in: [introduction, related-work]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Closest conceptual neighbor — both go beyond binary classification.\\\\n    EditLens = single-pass editing extent; ours = multi-generation depth.\\\\n\\\\nuchendu2020_attribution:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Authorship Attribution for Neural Text Generation\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Uchendu, Le, Shu, Lee]\\\\n  venue: EMNLP 2020\\\\n  year: 2020\\\\n  tags: [attribution, multi-source, authorship]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Multi-source attribution (which model?). We solve a different axis\\\\n    (how many iterations?) — complementary problems.\\\\n\\\\npu2026_generator_barrier:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Breaking the Generator Barrier: Disentangled Representation for Generalizable AI-Text Detection\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Pu, Cheng, Yuan, Wu, Bi]\\\\n  venue: arXiv preprint\\\\n  year: 2026\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2604.13692\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [disentanglement, cross-generator, generalization]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Cross-generator generalization via disentanglement.\\\\n    Analogous to our cross-model generalization challenge.\\\\n\\\\n# --- R2-N2 supplement: attribution paradigms ---\\\\n\\\\nbevendorff2025_two_paradigms:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"The Two Paradigms of LLM Detection: Authorship Attribution vs. Authorship Verification\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Bevendorff, Wiegmann, Richter, Potthast, Stein]\\\\n  venue: ACL 2025 Findings\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  tags: [attribution, verification, detection-paradigm, authorship]\\\\n  relevance: direct\\\\n  role: background\\\\n  verified: true\\\\n  integration: \\\\\\\"整合，'第三范式' framing（attribution/verification 之外的正交第三维度）\\\\\\\"\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Reframes LLM detection as authorship verification (not attribution).\\\\n    Our depth estimation is a third paradigm — ordinal characterization\\\\n    beyond binary verification or multi-class attribution. Key framing\\\\n    reference for positioning our contribution.\\\\n\\\\n# =============================================================\\\\n# METHOD COMPONENT — ordinal classification\\\\n# =============================================================\\\\n\\\\ncoral2020:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Rank Consistent Ordinal Regression for Neural Networks with Application to Age Estimation\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Cao, Mirjalili, Raschka]\\\\n  venue: Pattern Recognition Letters\\\\n  year: 2020\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"1901.07884\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [ordinal-classification, rank-consistency, cumulative-logits]\\\\n  relevance: direct\\\\n  cite_in: [method, experiments]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    CORAL ordinal classification framework used in our baseline battery.\\\\n    Rank-consistent cumulative logits for ordinal targets.\\\\n\\\\ncloc2025:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"CLOC: Contrastive Learning for Ordinal Classification with Multi-Margin N-pair Loss\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Pitawela, Carneiro, et al.]\\\\n  venue: CVPR 2025\\\\n  year: 2025\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2504.17813\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [contrastive-learning, ordinal, multi-margin, N-pair]\\\\n  relevance: direct\\\\n  cite_in: [method, dcol-section]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    DCOL is our adaptation of CLOC to text depth estimation.\\\\n    Multi-margin N-pair loss for ordinal-aware contrastive learning.\\\\n    Must cite as methodological basis for DCOL section.\\\\n\\\\n# =============================================================\\\\n# WATERMARKING\\\\n# =============================================================\\\\n\\\\nkirchenbauer2023_watermark:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"A Watermark for Large Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Kirchenbauer, Geiping, Wen, Katz, Miers, Goldstein]\\\\n  venue: ICML 2023\\\\n  year: 2023\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2301.10226\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [watermarking, proactive-detection, green-list]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [related-work]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Proactive detection via watermarking. Complementary —\\\\n    watermarking requires generator cooperation; our method is post-hoc.\\\\n\\\\n# =============================================================\\\\n# DATA CURATION\\\\n# =============================================================\\\\n\\\\ndsir2023:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Data Selection for Language Models via Importance Resampling\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Xie, Santurkar, Ma, Liang]\\\\n  venue: NeurIPS 2023\\\\n  year: 2023\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2302.03169\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [data-selection, importance-resampling, curation]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [discussion]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Quality-based data selection for LM training.\\\\n    Our depth estimation enables depth-graduated curation (Claim 3 / Discussion).\\\\n\\\\nqurating2024:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"QuRating: Selecting High-Quality Data for Training Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Wettig, Gupta, et al.]\\\\n  venue: ICML 2024\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2402.09739\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [quality-rating, data-curation, LLM-judgments]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  cite_in: [discussion]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    LLM-based quality rating for data selection.\\\\n    Our depth score is a complementary quality axis.\\\\n\\\\n# --- R2-N2 supplement: data curation at scale ---\\\\n\\\\npenedo2024_fineweb:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"The FineWeb Datasets: Decanting the Web for the Finest Text Data at Scale\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Penedo, Kydlicek, et al.]\\\\n  venue: NeurIPS 2024\\\\n  year: 2024\\\\n  arxiv: \\\\\\\"2406.17557\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tags: [data-curation, quality-filtering, deduplication, web-scale]\\\\n  relevance: indirect\\\\n  role: background\\\\n  cite_in: [discussion]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    15T token dataset with systematic quality filtering pipeline (dedup,\\\\n    heuristic filters, classifier-based selection). Our depth estimation\\\\n    adds a synthetic contamination axis complementary to FineWeb-style\\\\n    quality curation — depth score can flag recursively degraded text\\\\n    that passes standard quality filters.\\\\n\\\\n# =============================================================\\\\n# COVARIATE SHIFT — technical background\\\\n# =============================================================\\\\n\\\\nsugiyama2007_importance_weighting:\\\\n  title: \\\\\\\"Covariate Shift Adaptation by Importance Weighted Cross Validation\\\\\\\"\\\\n  authors: [Sugiyama, Krauledat, Mueller]\\\\n  venue: JMLR\\\\n  year: 2007\\\\n  tags: [covariate-shift, importance-weighting, domain-adaptation]\\\\n  relevance: background\\\\n  cite_in: [discussion]\\\\n  notes: >\\\\n    Theoretical foundation for covariate shift correction.\\\\n    Our z-score normalization is a simplified distribution alignment.\\\\n\\\\n# =============================================================\\\\n# COVERAGE CHECKLIST (paper_framing.md Dream D002 requirements)\\\\n# =============================================================\\\\n# [x] watermarking: kirchenbauer2023_watermark\\\\n# [x] model collapse theory: shumailov2024_nature, alemohammad2024_mad,\\\\n#     dohmatob2024_tale_of_tails, dohmatob2025_strong_collapse,\\\\n#     dohmatob2024_demystified, gerstgrasser2024_accumulate\\\\n# [x] model collapse empirical: drayson2025, bakshi2026, marchi2024,\\\\n#     shi2025_closer_look (VERIFIED), yi2026_escaping_collapse (VERIFIED, workshop)\\\\n# [x] data curation: dsir2023, qurating2024, penedo2024_fineweb (NEW)\\\\n# [x] multi-source attribution: uchendu2020_attribution,\\\\n#     bevendorff2025_two_paradigms (VERIFIED)\\\\n# [x] binary detectors: mitchell2023_detectgpt, sadasivan2023_paraphrase,\\\\n#     binoculars2024, fast_detectgpt2024, diveye2026, loki2025,\\\\n#     tulchinskii2024_intrinsic_dim (NEW), verma2024_ghostbuster (NEW),\\\\n#     li2024_mage (NEW)\\\\n# [x] detection survey: wu2025_detection_survey (VERIFIED)\\\\n# [x] decoding strategy effects: garces_arias2025, holtzman2020_nucleus,\\\\n#     wiher2022_decoding, basu2021_mirostat, finlayson2024_closing\\\\n# [x] ordinal classification: coral2020, cloc2025\\\\n# [x] adjacent tasks: editlens2026\\\\n# [x] covariate shift: sugiyama2007\\\\n# [x] statistical detection foundation: gehrmann2019_gltr\\\\n#\\\\n# Total unique entries: 43 (35 original + 8 R2-N2 supplement)\\\\n# 2024-2026 papers: 30/43 (70% recent coverage)\\\\n# Direct relevance: 12 entries\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 26296, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XYg221WCdZbKMQzBV1heER","session_id":"1446a884-2136-4659-bcf0-843e85ca00ce","uuid":"656c9963-8a97-4f18-aeb8-f3ff67c4bdd2","timestamp":"2026-05-17T09:00:41.425Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017Y9NW4v7N73sYJXG7y5yEk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JAYSbNrPJbNAcfoRXWETEp","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 Main conference submission. Your focus areas are **novelty/significance** and **related work positioning**.\n\n## ARR Scoring Scale\n- Overall: 1 (do not resubmit) to 5 (award-worthy). 4 = accept to conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = resubmit.\n- Soundness: 1 (major issues) to 5 (excellent). 4 = strong, 3 = acceptable, 2 = poor.\n- Excitement: 1 (not exciting) to 5 (highly exciting). 4 = exciting, 3 = interesting.\n- Confidence: 1-5. Reproducibility: 1-5.\n- Half-point increments allowed.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Characterizing Recursive Generation Depth: Feature-Based Estimation, Degradation Atlas, and Boundary Conditions\"\n\n**Core task**: Ordinal generation depth estimation — predicting how many recursive fine-tuning and re-generation cycles (depth d ∈ {0,1,2,3}) text has undergone, from text alone.\n\n**Method**: \n- Recursive generation protocol: 3 base models (Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-1B, GPT-2 XL) × 3 decoding strategies (nucleus p=0.95, temp τ=0.9, top-k k=50) × 4 depths (d=0..3). BOS-seeded unconditional generation, 498-token passages, 1000 passages per condition. LoRA fine-tuning (rank 16, α=32) at each depth iteration.\n- Feature extraction: 19 statistical features from a Pythia-160M reference model (6 perplexity stats, 5 surprisal stats, 4 lexical diversity, 4 distributional shape).\n- Classification: Random Forest (500 trees, max depth 12) and L2-regularized logistic regression, stratified 5-fold CV.\n- New metric: Effective Discriminable Depth (EDD) = max depth where all adjacent-depth AUC ≥ threshold T.\n\n**Key results**:\n- Controlled settings (within-strategy, BOS-seeded, 1-1.5B): 70-85% 4-class accuracy.\n- Depth Degradation Atlas findings: (i) perplexity tail statistics universally dominate; (ii) inter-depth distances are non-uniform, EDD=3 at T=0.75; (iii) temperature sampling causes feature-boundary reversal at depth-1/depth-2 interface (cross-strategy LOO d1v2 AUC collapses to 0.13).\n- Boundary conditions: 14B scale → 55% (EDD=1); prompted generation → 50% (EDD=1); web-crawl → 23-30% FPR.\n- DCOL end-to-end learning fails (6 variants, best 64.5% vs RF 78.2%).\n- Cross-strategy pipeline: strategy clf 57.5%, predicted two-stage (70.6%) < naive pooled (72.0%).\n\n**Contributions claimed**:\n1. Task formalization + benchmark\n2. Depth Degradation Atlas (primary)\n3. Boundary condition characterization\n4. Negative result on end-to-end learning\n\n**Related work sections**: Model Collapse & Recursive Training (Shumailov et al. 2024, Alemohammad et al. 2024, Dohmatob et al. 2024, Gerstgrasser et al. 2024, Drayson et al. 2025); Machine-Generated Text Detection (DetectGPT, Fast-DetectGPT, Binoculars, DivEye, LOKI, RADAR, authorship attribution); Watermarking (Kirchenbauer et al. 2023); Decoding Strategy Effects (Holtzman et al. 2020, Garcés Arias et al. 2025); Data Quality and Curation (DSIR, QuRating).\n\n**Reference list**: ~22 references total.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (3-4 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, with evidence)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, with evidence, distinguishing major vs minor)\n4. **Novelty assessment**: \n   - Is \"ordinal generation depth estimation\" genuinely a new task? How does it relate to model collapse characterization (which already studies depth-dependent degradation)?\n   - Is the Depth Degradation Atlas a novel methodological contribution or primarily a systematic experiment?\n   - Is the EDD metric novel and useful beyond this specific paper?\n   - Are any of the findings surprising given prior work on model collapse and AI-text detection?\n5. **Related work gaps**: \n   - Are important baselines missing? E.g., could existing detectors (Binoculars, Fast-DetectGPT) be adapted as baselines for depth estimation by calibrating their scores?\n   - Is the related work on data contamination / synthetic data detection sufficient?\n   - ~22 references seems low for EMNLP. What areas need more citations?\n   - Is there work on multi-class AI-text detection or fine-grained provenance that should be discussed?\n6. **Significance and impact**: Who benefits from this work? Is the practical impact convincing given the boundary conditions?\n7. **Presentation quality**: Is the paper well-written? Is the story coherent? Any structural issues?\n8. **Questions for authors** (up to 5)\n9. **Scores**: Overall, Soundness, Excitement, Confidence, Reproducibility (use ARR scale with half-points)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Consider: does this paper advance understanding of a real problem, or is it a niche exercise with narrow applicability?"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":830,"cache_read_input_tokens":55879,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":830},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1446a884-2136-4659-bcf0-843e85ca00ce","uuid":"c53f75b4-ebea-4b36-ab18-db48283eb90d"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a48c96b0a4b83f8d3","tool_use_id":"toolu_01JAYSbNrPJbNAcfoRXWETEp","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 Main conference submission. Your focus areas are **novelty/significance** and **related work positioning**.\n\n## ARR Scoring Scale\n- Overall: 1 (do not resubmit) to 5 (award-worthy). 4 = accept to conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = resubmit.\n- Soundness: 1 (major issues) to 5 (excellent). 4 = strong, 3 = acceptable, 2 = poor.\n- Excitement: 1 (not exciting) to 5 (highly exciting). 4 = exciting, 3 = interesting.\n- Confidence: 1-5. Reproducibility: 1-5.\n- Half-point increments allowed.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Characterizing Recursive Generation Depth: Feature-Based Estimation, Degradation Atlas, and Boundary Conditions\"\n\n**Core task**: Ordinal generation depth estimation — predicting how many recursive fine-tuning and re-generation cycles (depth d ∈ {0,1,2,3}) text has undergone, from text alone.\n\n**Method**: \n- Recursive generation protocol: 3 base models (Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-1B, GPT-2 XL) × 3 decoding strategies (nucleus p=0.95, temp τ=0.9, top-k k=50) × 4 depths (d=0..3). BOS-seeded unconditional generation, 498-token passages, 1000 passages per condition. LoRA fine-tuning (rank 16, α=32) at each depth iteration.\n- Feature extraction: 19 statistical features from a Pythia-160M reference model (6 perplexity stats, 5 surprisal stats, 4 lexical diversity, 4 distributional shape).\n- Classification: Random Forest (500 trees, max depth 12) and L2-regularized logistic regression, stratified 5-fold CV.\n- New metric: Effective Discriminable Depth (EDD) = max depth where all adjacent-depth AUC ≥ threshold T.\n\n**Key results**:\n- Controlled settings (within-strategy, BOS-seeded, 1-1.5B): 70-85% 4-class accuracy.\n- Depth Degradation Atlas findings: (i) perplexity tail statistics universally dominate; (ii) inter-depth distances are non-uniform, EDD=3 at T=0.75; (iii) temperature sampling causes feature-boundary reversal at depth-1/depth-2 interface (cross-strategy LOO d1v2 AUC collapses to 0.13).\n- Boundary conditions: 14B scale → 55% (EDD=1); prompted generation → 50% (EDD=1); web-crawl → 23-30% FPR.\n- DCOL end-to-end learning fails (6 variants, best 64.5% vs RF 78.2%).\n- Cross-strategy pipeline: strategy clf 57.5%, predicted two-stage (70.6%) < naive pooled (72.0%).\n\n**Contributions claimed**:\n1. Task formalization + benchmark\n2. Depth Degradation Atlas (primary)\n3. Boundary condition characterization\n4. Negative result on end-to-end learning\n\n**Related work sections**: Model Collapse & Recursive Training (Shumailov et al. 2024, Alemohammad et al. 2024, Dohmatob et al. 2024, Gerstgrasser et al. 2024, Drayson et al. 2025); Machine-Generated Text Detection (DetectGPT, Fast-DetectGPT, Binoculars, DivEye, LOKI, RADAR, authorship attribution); Watermarking (Kirchenbauer et al. 2023); Decoding Strategy Effects (Holtzman et al. 2020, Garcés Arias et al. 2025); Data Quality and Curation (DSIR, QuRating).\n\n**Reference list**: ~22 references total.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (3-4 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, with evidence)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, with evidence, distinguishing major vs minor)\n4. **Novelty assessment**: \n   - Is \"ordinal generation depth estimation\" genuinely a new task? How does it relate to model collapse characterization (which already studies depth-dependent degradation)?\n   - Is the Depth Degradation Atlas a novel methodological contribution or primarily a systematic experiment?\n   - Is the EDD metric novel and useful beyond this specific paper?\n   - Are any of the findings surprising given prior work on model collapse and AI-text detection?\n5. **Related work gaps**: \n   - Are important baselines missing? E.g., could existing detectors (Binoculars, Fast-DetectGPT) be adapted as baselines for depth estimation by calibrating their scores?\n   - Is the related work on data contamination / synthetic data detection sufficient?\n   - ~22 references seems low for EMNLP. What areas need more citations?\n   - Is there work on multi-class AI-text detection or fine-grained provenance that should be discussed?\n6. **Significance and impact**: Who benefits from this work? Is the practical impact convincing given the boundary conditions?\n7. **Presentation quality**: Is the paper well-written? Is the story coherent? Any structural issues?\n8. **Questions for authors** (up to 5)\n9. **Scores**: Overall, Soundness, Excitement, Confidence, Reproducibility (use ARR scale with half-points)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Consider: does this paper advance understanding of a real problem, or is it a niche exercise with narrow applicability?","uuid":"e2d17e37-e25c-45f9-b5e8-139a323a29af","session_id":"1446a884-2136-4659-bcf0-843e85ca00ce"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 Main conference submission. Your focus areas are **novelty/significance** and **related work positioning**.\n\n## ARR Scoring Scale\n- Overall: 1 (do not resubmit) to 5 (award-worthy). 4 = accept to conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = resubmit.\n- Soundness: 1 (major issues) to 5 (excellent). 4 = strong, 3 = acceptable, 2 = poor.\n- Excitement: 1 (not exciting) to 5 (highly exciting). 4 = exciting, 3 = interesting.\n- Confidence: 1-5. Reproducibility: 1-5.\n- Half-point increments allowed.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Characterizing Recursive Generation Depth: Feature-Based Estimation, Degradation Atlas, and Boundary Conditions\"\n\n**Core task**: Ordinal generation depth estimation — predicting how many recursive fine-tuning and re-generation cycles (depth d ∈ {0,1,2,3}) text has undergone, from text alone.\n\n**Method**: \n- Recursive generation protocol: 3 base models (Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-1B, GPT-2 XL) × 3 decoding strategies (nucleus p=0.95, temp τ=0.9, top-k k=50) × 4 depths (d=0..3). BOS-seeded unconditional generation, 498-token passages, 1000 passages per condition. LoRA fine-tuning (rank 16, α=32) at each depth iteration.\n- Feature extraction: 19 statistical features from a Pythia-160M reference model (6 perplexity stats, 5 surprisal stats, 4 lexical diversity, 4 distributional shape).\n- Classification: Random Forest (500 trees, max depth 12) and L2-regularized logistic regression, stratified 5-fold CV.\n- New metric: Effective Discriminable Depth (EDD) = max depth where all adjacent-depth AUC ≥ threshold T.\n\n**Key results**:\n- Controlled settings (within-strategy, BOS-seeded, 1-1.5B): 70-85% 4-class accuracy.\n- Depth Degradation Atlas findings: (i) perplexity tail statistics universally dominate; (ii) inter-depth distances are non-uniform, EDD=3 at T=0.75; (iii) temperature sampling causes feature-boundary reversal at depth-1/depth-2 interface (cross-strategy LOO d1v2 AUC collapses to 0.13).\n- Boundary conditions: 14B scale → 55% (EDD=1); prompted generation → 50% (EDD=1); web-crawl → 23-30% FPR.\n- DCOL end-to-end learning fails (6 variants, best 64.5% vs RF 78.2%).\n- Cross-strategy pipeline: strategy clf 57.5%, predicted two-stage (70.6%) < naive pooled (72.0%).\n\n**Contributions claimed**:\n1. Task formalization + benchmark\n2. Depth Degradation Atlas (primary)\n3. Boundary condition characterization\n4. Negative result on end-to-end learning\n\n**Related work sections**: Model Collapse & Recursive Training (Shumailov et al. 2024, Alemohammad et al. 2024, Dohmatob et al. 2024, Gerstgrasser et al. 2024, Drayson et al. 2025); Machine-Generated Text Detection (DetectGPT, Fast-DetectGPT, Binoculars, DivEye, LOKI, RADAR, authorship attribution); Watermarking (Kirchenbauer et al. 2023); Decoding Strategy Effects (Holtzman et al. 2020, Garcés Arias et al. 2025); Data Quality and Curation (DSIR, QuRating).\n\n**Reference list**: ~22 references total.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (3-4 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, with evidence)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, with evidence, distinguishing major vs minor)\n4. **Novelty assessment**: \n   - Is \"ordinal generation depth estimation\" genuinely a new task? How does it relate to model collapse characterization (which already studies depth-dependent degradation)?\n   - Is the Depth Degradation Atlas a novel methodological contribution or primarily a systematic experiment?\n   - Is the EDD metric novel and useful beyond this specific paper?\n   - Are any of the findings surprising given prior work on model collapse and AI-text detection?\n5. **Related work gaps**: \n   - Are important baselines missing? E.g., could existing detectors (Binoculars, Fast-DetectGPT) be adapted as baselines for depth estimation by calibrating their scores?\n   - Is the related work on data contamination / synthetic data detection sufficient?\n   - ~22 references seems low for EMNLP. What areas need more citations?\n   - Is there work on multi-class AI-text detection or fine-grained provenance that should be discussed?\n6. **Significance and impact**: Who benefits from this work? Is the practical impact convincing given the boundary conditions?\n7. **Presentation quality**: Is the paper well-written? Is the story coherent? Any structural issues?\n8. **Questions for authors** (up to 5)\n9. **Scores**: Overall, Soundness, Excitement, Confidence, Reproducibility (use ARR scale with half-points)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Consider: does this paper advance understanding of a real problem, or is it a niche exercise with narrow applicability?"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01JAYSbNrPJbNAcfoRXWETEp","session_id":"1446a884-2136-4659-bcf0-843e85ca00ce","uuid":"90cec23e-0fd4-47a9-808a-25b14cd1804b","timestamp":"2026-05-17T09:00:43.436Z"}
